Windows Registry Editor Version 5.00

; ElderSafety hardening for Google Chrome and Microsoft Edge (Windows 10/11).
; Run once from an administrator account: double-click → Yes. Undo with browser-policies-remove.reg.
; Check the result at chrome://policy and edge://policy (click "Reload policies").
; Afterwards the browsers will say "Managed by your organization" — that is expected.

; ---------------- Google Chrome ----------------
[HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SOFTWARE\Policies\Google\Chrome]
; Websites can no longer ask to show notifications (the #1 source of fake "virus" pop-ups).
"DefaultNotificationsSetting"=dword:00000002
; Enhanced Safe Browsing: stronger, real-time checks against known scam and malware sites.
"SafeBrowsingProtectionLevel"=dword:00000002
; Red "Deceptive site ahead" / "Dangerous download" warnings can't be clicked through.
"DisableSafeBrowsingProceedAnyway"=dword:00000001
; Block downloads Chrome considers dangerous (1). Use 4 for "malicious only" if this gets in the way.
"DownloadRestrictions"=dword:00000001

; ---------------- Microsoft Edge ----------------
[HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Edge]
"DefaultNotificationsSetting"=dword:00000002
"SmartScreenEnabled"=dword:00000001
; Also block "potentially unwanted apps" (toolbars, fake cleaners, bundled junk).
"SmartScreenPuaEnabled"=dword:00000001
; SmartScreen warnings can't be clicked through, for sites or for downloaded files.
"PreventSmartScreenPromptOverride"=dword:00000001
"PreventSmartScreenPromptOverrideForFiles"=dword:00000001
"DownloadRestrictions"=dword:00000001
